# Preserve original asset identities, package relationships, and ordering

Preserve original asset identities, package relationships, and ordering.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Preserve original asset identities, package relationships, and ordering.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

World scenegraph placement collection now preserves authored traversal order and
authored multiplicity instead of sorting placements by transform bits and
deduplicating identical matrices. The normalized P3D decoders already preserve
scenegraph child order and emit each contained instance graph once, so the extra
post-collection sorting/deduplication was not source authority. Two focused
regressions
prove equal source transforms remain two placements and a source order of 9 then
1 remains 9 then 1. The full pipeline library suite passes with 802 tests and
strict pipeline Clippy is clean.

World component-ledger intake now rejects duplicate component ordinals instead
of silently accepting or deduplicating malformed rows. Mesh sources are ordered
only by their explicit source component ordinal; owner kind/name no longer
reorders them before coordinate matching and FBX assembly. Focused regressions
prove source ordinals 11 then 21 remain in that order even when owner names sort
the opposite way, and duplicate nested ordinal 2 fails closed. The complete
pipeline library now passes 804 tests with strict Clippy clean.

World export no longer infers new mesh identities from component proximity. The
old one-unit Blender grouping threshold could split one decoded source mesh into
multiple generated objects solely because its disconnected geometry was far
apart. Source mesh ownership is now preserved at every distance, while explicit
source owner and placement metadata still carries independent/breakable/
interactable roles.

Auxiliary coordinate-reference P3Ds no longer match canonical meshes by owner
alone or topology alone. A donor must have the same source owner kind/name, the
same mesh identity, and compatible topology inside the same package identity;
multiple exact donors fail closed. Focused tests prove owner-only and
topology-only references are ignored while one exact donor remains admissible.

World interaction roles now follow exact decoded container kinds rather than
mesh/name substring inference. `srr_tree_dsg` and `srr_breakable_object` remain
breakable, while dynamic/static-physics owners remain interactable even if their
mesh name contains `tree`. A focused regression preserves that distinction.

World component-ledger intake now also requires each normalized component path
to be unique in addition to its source ordinal. Two rows cannot claim one
component/member identity under different ordinals; a focused regression proves
duplicate `mesh/shared.json` ownership fails before mesh association.
Every grouped component must also resolve its declared root-owner ordinal after
the complete ledger is parsed. Orphan mesh rows no longer disappear through a
silent downstream skip; a focused regression proves missing owner ordinal 99
fails closed before package-mesh publication. Direct root-owner rows
additionally
must self-own: their `container_ordinal` must equal their own source ordinal,
matching the P3D extractor's deterministic top-level-ancestor contract. A
focused regression rejects a root ordinal 1 that attempts to claim container 2.
World-level mesh discovery also validates the extractor's exact relative path
contract: a row labeled `mesh` must resolve as `mesh/<member>.json` before that
member can be loaded from the mesh component directory. A focused regression
rejects `kind: mesh` provenance that instead names `shader/shared.json`.
Ledger intake now consumes the extractor's `parent_ordinal` ancestry evidence as
well: direct root owners must declare parent 0, while nested published
components
must declare a non-root parent. Focused regressions reject a depth-1 owner whose
parent is 2 and a depth-2 mesh that incorrectly names root parent 0. When a
published child also has its immediate parent present in the ledger, intake now
requires exactly one depth step and the same top-level container. Focused
regressions reject both a depth-3 child of a depth-1 parent and a child whose
published parent belongs to container 1 while the child claims container 2.
P3D normalized-package cache validation now also treats recovered component
paths by portable case-insensitive identity. A manifest cannot declare both
`components/mesh.json` and `COMPONENTS/MESH.json` as distinct complete
artifacts, matching the repository filesystem boundary on every host. P3D
extraction now applies the same identity before filesystem publication: only a
byte-identical nested repeat of the exact same path may reuse an already
published artifact. Different nested payloads, duplicate direct-root paths, and
case-equivalent paths fail closed instead of dropping or overwriting evidence.
World prop inventory now also rejects two distinct mesh members that decode to
the same source mesh identity instead of letting `BTreeMap` insertion silently
choose one member before composite association. This matches the existing
non-world component-map fail-closed identity rule. World component-ledger
intake now applies the same portable case-insensitive path identity used by P3D
publication and cache validation, while retaining a distinct diagnostic for an
exact duplicate path. A focused regression rejects `mesh/Shared.json` alongside
`mesh/shared.json` before either member can reach world assembly. P3D cache
admission now requires the ancestry fields emitted by `component_line` and
validates the same root/nested relationship invariants before reusing normalized
artifacts. When an immediate published parent is present, depth and top-level
container continuity must also agree; omitted intermediate chunks remain valid.
Normalized P3D cache reuse is now bound to the current exact raw source bytes:
new package headers record `source_sha256`, and the batch exporter skips a
complete cache only when that local digest matches the current input. Replacing
a source P3D in place therefore forces regeneration instead of reusing stale
normalized evidence; no source path is added to the manifest. Cache reuse now
also binds the exact normalized `source.p3d` artifact separately through
`normalized_sha256`, computed from the prepared bytes actually written by the
extractor. Missing or corrupted normalized source evidence forces regeneration
even when the raw input and component files still match; a filesystem regression
proves both corruption and deletion are rejected. The same normalized-artifact
check now replays the manifest's declared `byte_len` against the exact verified
`source.p3d` length, so a cache cannot remain current after editing that header
fact independently of the artifact digest. Each published primary component row
now also records the SHA-256 of the exact JSON/image bytes written beneath
`components/`. Source-aware cache reuse requires those digests to match in
addition to payload validity, so replacing one component with different valid
JSON or image bytes forces regeneration instead of silently accepting the edit.
Normalized-source verification now parses the exact verified `source.p3d` as a
P3D document and replays both parser-derived byte length and chunk count against
the manifest header. An edited `chunk_count` therefore cannot remain current
merely because the normalized artifact digest still matches. Source-aware cache
admission now also binds each published row's parser-derived provenance back to
that exact normalized document: ordinal lookup, depth, immediate parent,
top-level container, and decoded chunk kind must agree. A regression proves an
internally valid `texture` row cannot replace a source `mesh` row.

Non-world and world composite-prop selection now preserves the authored
`props` array order instead of converting references through `BTreeSet` and
sorting/deduplication normalization. Composite intake also rejects duplicate
cleaned prop
identities rather than silently collapsing repeated evidence that the current
outbound model cannot represent independently. Focused prop-catalog regressions
prove a source order of `zebra`, `alpha`, `middle` remains unchanged and padded
duplicate `shared` identities fail closed; all 84 prop-catalog tests pass.

FBX character shader resolution preserves the canonical package-member input
sequence instead of adding another alphabetical sort or silently deduplicating
file-stem identities. Duplicate shader identities fail closed. This resolution
order is not claimed as authored source order: binary FBX material slots are
separately derived from first primitive-group use as described below.

Character rigid prop meshes now reconstruct source order from preserved chunk
ordinals before aggregate assembly. The current character corpus has one package
where this is observable: `hibber_m` indexes its three prop meshes at source
ordinals 181, 170, and 192 in canonical path order, so assembly now restores
170, 181, 192 before those parts receive geometry identities.

Character animation-bank paths now use the same fail-closed source-ordinal
projection before clip loading. The current corpus contains 117 character
packages with 482 animation members, including 13 multi-animation packages; all
13 currently happen to match source-ordinal order. The explicit projection still
matters because request path order becomes clip order and the first clip selects
the active FBX animation stack.

Vehicle mesh, billboard, and animation source projection now reconstructs exact
source chunk order from preserved member ordinals instead of treating canonical
role/path/id index position as authored order. Missing or duplicate selected
ordinals and duplicate projected paths fail closed at the vehicle source
boundary.

The current car corpus has 535 mesh, 716 quad-group, and 105 animation members
with numeric source ordinals and no duplicate ordinals inside a package. Among
multi-member packages, 85 of 88 mesh sequences and all 84 quad-group sequences
differ from source-ordinal order, while all 22 animation sequences currently
coincide. Animation projection still uses the explicit ordinal because clip
order feeds FBX animation order and its first clip becomes the active stack.

Billboard quad-group decoding now preserves each authored child-quad identity
through the typed primitive-group boundary and emits it as FBX user metadata
without changing deterministic FBX object names. The normalized corpus contains
3,139 quads across 2,378 groups; 837 child names differ from their group name,
while only two groups repeat a child name internally, so the child name remains
provenance rather than a unique object key. No audited group, shader, or child
identity depends on surrounding-space trimming, and the decoder now rejects
such padding while retaining only fixed-width trailing NUL removal. Billboard
mode, depth, range, and other presentation semantics remain separate conversion
evidence and are not claimed complete by this identity fix.

Binary FBX material planning now preserves first-use order from the canonical
primitive-group sequence when deduplicating exact semantic variants. Material
object ids, object emission, and texture connections no longer inherit lexical
`BTreeMap` key order; a focused binary artifact regression proves `zebra_m`
remains before `alpha_m` when that is their first source-group use order.

Binary FBX skin-cluster planning now uses influence identities only for
membership and projects that membership back through the canonical skeleton
sequence. Cluster objects and connections therefore retain source skeleton
ordinals instead of lexical bone-name order; a focused regression proves a
`z_root` parent remains before its lexically earlier `a_child` child.

Decoded animation helper-group evidence now retains decoded source-group order
alongside keyed lookup. Unbound helper identities no longer inherit lexical map
order; the compact-channel fixture proves `Z_Helper` remains before
`A_Helper` when that is the source sequence.

Prop canonicalization now preserves the supplied source mesh and rigid-part
sequence while assigning anonymous `part-####` publication names. Content-based
sorting previously changed `z`-then-`a` source parts into `a`-then-`z` before
FBX publication and made those distinct authored sequences semantically
equivalent.
Static and rigid-animated regressions now prove canonical naming leaves the
source part sequence unchanged; all 86 focused prop-catalog tests and the 1,002
pipeline library tests pass with strict pipeline Clippy clean.

World structural-guide assembly now concatenates the accumulated
`MasterContent` mesh vector directly instead of sorting it by mesh name before
flattening geometry. A focused regression supplies `z` then `a` meshes with
distinct source positions and proves the first published triangle still comes
from `z`; the full pipeline library now passes 1,003 tests with strict pipeline
Clippy clean.

World FBX publication now preserves the accumulated source mesh sequence through
the binary writer instead of sorting by mesh name immediately before export. A
focused `z` then `a` regression proves `write_content_fbx` leaves that sequence
unchanged. Interior fusion now also keeps the exact positional pairing captured
between render meshes and their cloned ownership geometry, retaining the
fail-closed identity check without sorting both lists. Focused regressions cover
both boundaries; the full pipeline library now passes 1,005 tests with strict
pipeline Clippy clean.

The exact world-level input selector currently finds 129 extracted packages
with component ledgers. Of 123 multi-mesh packages, 120 have source-ordinal mesh
order that differs from lexical mesh-name order, covering 9,765 source meshes
across those multi-mesh packages. This is input-order evidence only; downstream
filtering and reviewed algorithms retain their separate contracts.

Prop animation canonicalization now retains decoded helper-group identities that
are intentionally unbound to deforming skeleton bones. The decoder already
preserves those validated identities in source-group order; canonicalization no
longer clears them before `PreparedGeometry` enters semantic signature hashing.
A focused regression proves `Z_Helper` then `A_Helper` remains unchanged while
exportable clip and bone identities are canonicalized; the full pipeline library
now passes 1,006 tests.

Package-member mirrors now preserve the exact source chunk ordinal already
carried by the normalized minor-unit manifest instead of dropping it between
phase-two classification and phase-three intake. The current mirror accepts a
positive decimal ordinal or explicit `none`; historical six-field mirrors remain
readable as unavailable ordinal provenance, while malformed and zero current
ordinals fail closed. The normalized corpus has 132,179 chunk-backed indexed
members, and all 132,179 carry numeric source ordinals; 29,781 loose members
carry `none`, with no missing or invalid manifest joins.

This ordinal makes same-name package-local billboard occurrences independently
addressable without promoting filenames or names to relationship authority. In
`l5r3`, the duplicated light-pool and light-beam rows resolve exactly as source
ordinals 5311/5315 under container 5299 and 5453/5457 under container 5441 when
rejoined to the package component ledger. `container_ordinal` remains canonical
component-ledger evidence and is not yet claimed as a direct package-member
mirror.

The package-member vector itself remains the canonical phase-two role/path/id
order rather than claiming authored chunk order. A corpus audit finds 1,956
packages with at least two chunk-backed members, and 1,919 of those sequences
differ from ascending source chunk ordinal. Consumers that require authored
ordering must therefore project the preserved ordinal explicitly at a reviewed
boundary instead of treating canonical index position as source order.

Scrooby layout publication now validates source component relationships before
constructing parent and sibling maps. Duplicate component ordinals, malformed
`parent_ordinal` values, and non-root parents that do not resolve to a published
component all fail closed instead of becoming last-write-wins or null runtime
ownership. The current normalized corpus has 26 Scrooby ledgers with 13,309
Scrooby rows: all ordinals are unique, all 13,283 non-root parents resolve, and
all parent fields are numeric while the 26 root rows retain parent ordinal 0.

This umbrella task remains open for broader asset/package identity and ordering
evidence across the remaining conversion surfaces.
